hi there this is steve kaufman i'm not  at home i'm uh on holiday  uh i had two weeks in china  magnificent weeks  my country's just amazing the amount of  construction  somebody said that the national bird of  china is the construction crane crane  get it bird crane you just see  construction cranes everywhere  the density of construction in  even smaller towns  the i traveled on the high-speed train  it's magnificent there's phenomenal  radio rail stations in every  town it it just blows the mind and  for someone who lived there  or at least didn't live there but  visited there in the late 60s early 70s  to see what's happening in china today  is just beyond belief  and of course it all started to happen  insofar as economic growth was concerned  once the um  you know the  free action of the market once private  initiative was allowed to uh  to  participate in the in economic activity  in china prior to that they had  all kinds of public  uh initiatives mass mobilization of the  people  based on whatever  theory  was popular with the government at that  time the government made all the  decisions  um  and so people are mobilized with spades  to go and dig  lengthy you know irrigation ditches that  may or may not have been useful  um  backyard  furnaces and whatever they did i can't  remember but the net result was a lot of  wasted energy wasted resources suffering  people dying unnecessarily and so forth  and uh in the last uh  30 years the country has just progressed  economically  tremendously now it has created a lot of  inequality in the country and i think  that's something they're trying to  address now  but it has created tremendous wealth  tremendous wealth  so many people are well off well to do  sitting in the train with people who  they've all got ipads the people that  i'm sitting with now of course there are  very poor people who are working largely  on piecework 14 hours a day but there no  one's complaining that i can see they're  all working very hard to try and and and  improve their lot and so you take 1.3  billion people or a large number of them  working very very hard  and you have a tremendous economic  engine  but i was thinking about this whole  issue of public and private  because we have a tremendous crisis now  in in the western world  where we have countries that have  enormous debt  and  public sectors that are feel they are  entitled to  um  you know as a as almost like a  birthright  to certain uh conditions of work and  compensation  and uh  by the same token people want to avoid  paying taxes in some countries i guess  particularly in places like greece  but uh countries and and in italy and  and elsewhere but also in the united  states and so forth countries have  been running these enormous enormous  deficits  so i don't know in detail how  useful a lot of public expenditure is  it's it's certainly true that it's hard  to argue against things like  you know publicly like that  the whole society pays for medical care  the whole society pays for health care  for for education that we have public  parks public facilities that all these  things are available free of charge even  that the medical system is available  free of charge it's a very popular idea  i myself like the idea that we have  public facilities that we can use  without having to pay for that's a great  idea  if we can afford them  the the problem is that the size of  government and the the  and of course i was just looking at our  own situation where i live in vancouver  in our municipal government in west  vancouver 80 percent of the cost is in  wages  the wage package and the compensation  package that those people have is much  higher than the corresponding level in  the private sector  uh the size of our local government has  grown  by 40 percent in the last eight or nine  years when inflation has been very low  i don't think any citizens are aware of  any improvement in services  but  these organizations just naturally grow  they feel have to do this they have to  hire that person this person it all  grows  if i look at  language education for immigrants  which costs canada about a billion  dollars a year sort of instruction in  the official languages primarily for  immigrants but also this uh  i mean i recently read in the paper that  a senior bureaucrat was appointed to  some position  and uh he was in his 50s i guess and and  so the government decided that he was  going to get  one or two years of full-time french  instruction  uh the man probably earns well over a  hundred thousand dollars he's going to  earn his salary and he's going to have  full-time  language instruction in this particular  article i read in the paper  it said that  uh you know language instruction you  know intensive language instruction can  cost up to a hundred thousand dollars a  year in auto i mean that's absolutely  mind-blowing  uh a high percentage of people within  the civil service who take these  language courses in french never ever  end up using the language  but if i get back to the immigrants i  ask myself the question if there were no  if there was no government-supported  language instruction for immigrants what  what would happen would there be any  difference  i think that there wouldn't be much  difference  in terms of  the uh  you know rate at which immigrants learn  english or french i mean let that ring  um  those immigrants  now who want to learn  they learn  they find a way to learn and if you're  living in canada let's say if you're  living in vancouver you have the radio  you have television you have newspapers  uh you have all the resources that are  available on the internet for learning  languages you can go buy a book you can  go to the library you can borrow  materials you can borrow rosetta stone  there's so much available if you're  motivated to learn not to mention just  talking to people  people who are not motivated motivated  to learn won't learn so you can send  them to school and we see them now the  immigrants gather  you know every morning for their two  hour class  uh and then go back home and spend the  rest of the day in their native language  and they don't learn  so i'm quite convinced that  if immigrants were told language  improvement is your own responsibility  you have to do it maybe even held out  some  you know competition or reward but even  without that just tell them it's your  job to do it  we expect you to do it but  you no one can help you you have to do  it on your own you have to want to do it  if you want to do it you can do it if  you don't want to do it you aren't going  to be able to do it whether you go to  school or not  i think  that there would be  little difference in outcomes  that all of the money we spend on  schools in this public education uh of  immigrants in the official languages in  canada  is  largely  a waste of money  that  it's not a waste of money in the sense  that there are teachers earning salaries  from this but in terms of the net effect  in terms of the number of people who  come to canada and who learn to speak  english well or french  i don't think it has much impact and i'd  be very interested to hear your opinion  and i'm sure that most people don't  agree with me so  look forward to hearing from you bye for  now
 

